. do "20250731_141921_01ygl2_h1.do"

. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. ********************** Testing H1: #ManyDaughters Project *******************
> ***
. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. 
. * Authors: [REDACTED] & [REDACTED]  
. 
. *----------------------------------------------------------------------------
> ------*
. * H1. Having daughters leads to stronger support for gender equality (as meas
> ured by attitudes).
. *----------------------------------------------------------------------------
> ------*
. 
. /* Verbal description:
> 
>                 Assumptions
>                 *----------------------------           
>                 
>                 Our strategy compares parents of boys to parents of girls. Th
> is approach has
>                 two implicit assumptions. First, that parents are not a rando
> m subsample. So, 
>                 direct comparisons of parents and non-parents does not make m
> uch sense. 
>                 Second, we assume that the gender of the newborn is random. I
> n other words, we pressume
>                 selective abortion to be rare / non existent in Germany. Give
> n these assumptions, 
>                 potantial outcomes are independent of the gender of the newbo
> rn. So, a 
>                 difference in means suffices to identify the effect of parent
> ing a daughter.
>                 
>                 Initially, we wanted to restrict the sample to individuals wh
> o have just had
>                 their first children. Unfortunately, this was not possible, a
> s the number
>                 of children born in each year is relatively small, and (more 
> importantly)
>                 some women might be already expecting a second child. 
>                 
>                 So, we opted to rely on the randomness of the gender of the f
> irst born to 
>                 identify the effect of parenting a daughter. The regression d
> oes not include 
>                 any additional variables that were realized after the birth o
> f the first child, 
>                 as these variables are likely endogenous to the model. Perhap
> s parents decide
>                 to spend more time at home if the child is a "girl" ( or a "b
> oy"), in 
>                 which case controlling for thse variables will block the true
>  effect. 
>                 Controlling for the number of children is also problematic, a
> s this variable
>                 is also endogeneous to the gender of the first child. Perhaps
>  more conservative 
>                 couples have a preference for boys, which means that the subs
> ample of 
>                 respondents with a single daughter has a different distributi
> on of unobservables
>                 (traditional views) than families with only one son. 
>         
>                                 
>                 Implementation
>                 *------------------
>                 
>                 The survey contains several variables that reflect gender nor
> ms
>                 (e.g. whether children suffers when one parent works). Unfort
> unately these variables
>                 are collected in one survey, which limits the options availab
> le. 
>                 
>                 Instead of evaluating variables one by one, we use PCA to red
> uce 
>                 the dimensionality to the first eigenvalue for the main regre
> ssion. In this 
>                 new variable higher values indicate tendency to greater gende
> r equality. 
>                 A complete analysis would also entail looking at each compone
> nt separately.
>                 
>                 The following variables were used in the PCA. All data was co
> llected in 2012
>                 * plh0298_v1: Child under 6 suffers if mother works (FID) [20
> 12]
>                 * plh0302_v1: Child under 3 suffers if mother works (FID) [20
> 12]
>                 * plh0308_v1: Family: Man and woman should share equally in w
> ork and household/family duties
>                 * plh0301: Women should focus more on the family than on thei
> r career
>                 
>                 We also introduce some sample restrictions. 
>                         - We include only parents at the time of the survey (
> based on date of birth of first child)
>                         - We focus on couples headed by a German national (va
> riable pop). 
>                         - We exclude individuals who did not answer all quest
> ions on gender attitudes
>                         - We exclude individuals with a unique year of birth 
> of the first child (required for SE)
> 
>                 In the regression we also include the year of birth of the fi
> rstborn as 
>                 an additional control. The goal is to restrict comparisons to
>  parents of children
>                 of the same age. 
>                 
>                         */
.         
. * ============================
. * Setup * 
. * ============================
. 
. * clear all
. version 16 // indicate the version of stata you are using here

. set maxvar 10000 // extend the maximum number of variables to 10,000


. 
. 
. * CONFIGURE FILE PATHS * (Please do not change)
. /*
> * the following code automatically sets paths based on where you saved the di
> stributed file-package on your system *
